00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:28,840
What makes humans different from other species?

161
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:35,220
Some philosophers argue it's morals or ethics, while some scientists assert it's our

162
00:14:35,220 --> 00:14:36,860
greater cognitive development.

163
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:41,980
But I argue that the main difference is our desire to combat routine.

164
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:47,880
This makes being creative a biological mandate, as what we seek in art and

165
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:52,000
technology is surprise, not simply a fulfillment of expectations.

166
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:58,620
As a result, a wild imagination has characterized the history of our species.

167
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:05,660
We build intricate habitats, devise complex recipes for our food, wear clothes that

168
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:07,820
reflect constant changes in fashion,

169
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:14,740
communicate with elaborate signs, symbols and sounds, and travel between habitats on

170
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:17,280
wings and wheels of our own design.

171
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:22,020
To satisfy our appetite for novelty, innovation is key.

172
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:23,600
But who innovates?

173
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:30,480
Now, many people, both laymen and experts, believe that only geniuses innovate.

174
00:15:31,020 --> 00:15:35,480
But I believe that innovation is not something that only a people do.

175
00:15:35,940 --> 00:15:41,200
The innovative drive lives in every human brain, and the resulting war against the

176
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:45,960
repetitive is what powers the massive changes that distinguish one generation from

177
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:50,980
the next. The drive to create the new is a trait of being human.

178
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:55,620
We build cultures by the hundreds and tell new stories by the millions.

179
00:15:56,360 --> 00:16:01,560
We create and surround ourselves with things that have never existed before, while

180
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:02,820
animals do not.

181
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:06,400
But where do our new ideas come from?

182
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:11,780
According to many, new ideas come from seemingly nowhere, to great minds.

183
00:16:11,780 --> 00:16:16,100
From this perspective, new ideas are almost like magic.

184
00:16:16,620 --> 00:16:20,040
They come in a flash of inspiration to a select few.

185
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:26,860
However, the reality is that, across the spectrum of human activities, prior work

186
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:28,800
propels the creative process.

187
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:35,020
We may think of innovation as being the result of inspiration or genius, but it's

188
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:38,680
really the result of developing the ideas of others further.

189
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:44,220
This happens in technology, where one invention enables or inspires further

190
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:50,420
inventions. And it happens in the arts, as writers, composers, and painters use the

191
00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:53,200
work of previous artists in their own work.

192
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:56,460
The human brain works from precedent.

193
00:16:56,940 --> 00:17:01,100
We take the ideas we've inherited and put them together into some new shape.

194
00:17:01,780 --> 00:17:03,280
What is a creator?

195
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:06,840
Is a creator a genius who makes something out of nothing?

196
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:14,060
No. Creators are simply humans who use what they inherit, who absorb the past and

197
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:16,640
manipulate it to create possible futures.

198
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:22,180
Thus, humans are creators as a rule, rather than as an exception.

